    meditation: `Open the contract drawer of any working lawyer and you will find — under the boilerplate, the recitals, the integration clauses, the limitations on consequential damages — a series of small moral artifacts. Each began as a sentence one human being said to another: I will deliver the goods by the fifteenth. I will not solicit your employees for two years. I will pay the principal back in monthly installments. I will indemnify you against third-party claims arising from my negligence. Then the lawyers came and the sentence acquired vocabulary, qualifications, governing-law clauses, signature blocks, exhibits. But the sentence is still inside it. Strip away the apparatus and you reach the original moral act: a word given, freely, by a person who could have remained silent.

Aquinas, in the article above, says something the modern lawyer is not always trained to notice. Faith-keeping — the virtue by which one keeps one's word — is a virtue of justice. Not a virtue of kindness, not a virtue of religious devotion, not a counsel of perfection for the saints. A virtue of justice, the cardinal virtue, the one whose business is rendering to each what is his due. Aquinas's reasoning is precise: a man owes fidelity to him to whom he has bound himself by his word. The promise creates a debt. The debt is owed. Justice — that constant and perpetual will to render each their due — therefore requires the keeping of the promise. To break it without sufficient cause is, in its own kind, a sin against justice.

This is not the romantic view. The romantic view treats promises as effusions of the heart and breaches as tragic but mainly aesthetic failures — disappointments rather than wrongs. The Thomistic view is more austere and, on examination, more dignifying. When you give your word, you have created in another person a rightful expectation. That expectation is now part of their estate. They have planned around it, ordered goods on the strength of it, made representations to third parties trusting it, declined other offers because of it. To breach the promise is to remove something that was theirs. It is, in the most rigorous sense, a taking.

This is the moral substrate of the document the junior associate is redlining at midnight. The warranty paragraph the client wants softened — the one where the seller represents that the goods will conform to spec for twelve months — is not a marketing flourish. It is a promise. The settlement agreement with the release that is too broad has, hiding in its operative paragraph, a promise the plaintiff will not pursue claims he does not yet know he has, on the strength of a payment that does not yet take account of the medical bills he has not yet received. The loan covenant the founder forgot — the one that requires audited financials by April 30 — is a promise made to a banker eighteen months ago by a younger version of the founder who did not foresee the cap-table fight that would consume April. Each is a moral artifact, and each is now under your hand.

The temptation, in the daily life of a transactional lawyer, is to treat these artifacts as mere allocations of risk. The MBAs talk that way. Contracts, they will tell you with cheerful candor, are pricing mechanisms; they distribute liability across the parties best positioned to bear it; the question for the drafter is not whether the promise will be kept but whether the consequences of breach are appropriately allocated to the breacher. There is something to this — the law of contract damages does, in its operation, monetize the breach, and the famous Holmesian quip that a contract is nothing but a duty to perform or pay damages has not lost its grip on the bar. But the quip is half the story at most. Holmes was making a pedagogical point about how courts reason, not a moral point about what a promise is. A man who systematically treats his promises as priced options to breach has not understood Holmes; he has missed Aquinas.

Pay attention to what Aquinas adds. He says faith-keeping is a sin against justice when broken "without sufficient cause." The cause matters. The Thomistic tradition has always been ready to specify the circumstances under which a promise no longer binds, or binds differently, or must yield to a higher claim. We will spend much of this week on those circumstances — pacta sunt servanda and its limits, the role of mercy in release, the difference between a covenant and an option. But the framework matters. The default is that the promise binds. The departures from the default require reasons. The reasoned departures are what mercy and equity exist to identify. The unreasoned departures are what justice calls sins.

The lawyer's particular position in all this is delicate and honorable at once. You did not make the promise. You are not the obligor. You are the scribe — the one who reduces the obligor's word to a form the court can enforce, the one who later helps him understand what he committed to, the one who, when circumstances change, helps him think about how to keep faith or how to seek release. You are, in a quite specific sense, the keeper of other people's words. The contract drawer is your sacristy. The artifacts you preserve there are not yours, but they are in your custody.

This week reframes the daily work of contract. Read your transactional life with Aquinas's framing on the desk. Every clause you negotiate is a promise being made or qualified. Every breach letter you draft is a charge that a promise has been broken — that someone is, in his own kind, sinning against justice, or that he believes he has sufficient cause and that you must hear it. Every release you draft is a forgiveness — a promise to forbear from pressing what you would have a right to press. Every novation is a renewal. Every waiver is a small act of mercy, sometimes wisely given, sometimes not.

This is heavier than the M&A timeline lets on. It is also, if you can hold the gravity without becoming pious about it, ennobling. The contract drafter is doing real moral work — not making the promise, but giving it bones so that the promise can stand up when the parties' memories soften. The lawyer who treats this as paperwork is missing the fact that paperwork, at this level, is how a community remembers what was said.

We will spend the week with the various textures: covenant and contract, the promise that holds when the world changes, the promise mercy releases, the divine promise that anchors all the others, and the strange office of the lawyer as the institution that keeps faith on behalf of people who have moved on. Begin today by looking at one contract on your desk and asking: whose word is inside this document, and what does justice require I do with it?

The clause is more than its language. The clause is somebody's pledge, written down so it can be remembered. You are its custodian.`,
