    meditation: `Open the law and walk backwards through its history and you arrive, eventually, at Genesis 17. Not because the chapter contains anything we would recognize as a statute, but because what happens between God and Abraham there is the form of which every later contract is a variation. A party with power approaches a party with less. A relationship is initiated by words spoken. Obligations are taken on. A name is changed to mark the new status. A sign — circumcision — is established to memorialize the transaction in the body of the lesser party. The Hebrew word for this is berith. The Greek translators of the Septuagint rendered it diathēkē. The Vulgate calls it foedus or testamentum. The English word is covenant. The Anglo-American word that descends from it, attenuated and secularized, is contract.

The covenant is the original legal form. Everything that comes later — the Roman stipulatio, the medieval bond, the modern bilateral contract, the SaaS terms-of-service screen — is variation on a theme stated definitively at Mamre.

Notice the elements. There is a promisor and a promisee. There is consideration of a sort — Abraham is not paying God, but he is walking before God and being perfect, and his descendants will be circumcised, and the relationship is, in the deepest sense, mutual. There is a duration: perpetual covenant, kept generation to generation. There is a benefit running to the promisee: the land of his sojournment, descendants, divine favor. There is a sign that distinguishes those who are inside the agreement from those who are outside. There is a remedy assumed — God will be Israel's God, and Israel will be reckoned as God's people, and the failure of either to keep faith will be marked by a withdrawal of the relationship's benefits. This is, in legal vocabulary that did not yet exist, a recognizable contractual structure. It is just that the parties are not equal, the consideration is asymmetric, and the duration extends past the death of any individual signatory.

This is why the deep tradition of Western law has never treated contract as a merely commercial phenomenon. The first contract — the prototype — was theological. The first promisor was God. The first promisee was a ninety-nine-year-old patriarch on his face in the dust of the Negev. The legal form is downstream of this scene; the courthouse and the closing-room are imitations, in lower keys, of what happened first there.

A lawyer who keeps this in view reads his daily work differently. The seven-year supply agreement is, in its essence, a covenant of limited scope. The shareholders' agreement is a covenant defining a community. The marriage that became a divorce — and you have done one or more — was, before anything else, a covenant. The bankruptcy reorganization plan is a new covenant offered to creditors whose old covenants the debtor could not perform. The treaty between nations is a covenant. The constitution is a covenant. Even the deeply attenuated covenant of a credit card click-through still belongs, faintly, to the tradition that began with God promising land to a sojourner.

This is not sentimentality. The tradition was theorized this way by people who built the common law. The Anglo-Saxon wed — the pledge by which a bondsman gave a token, often a literal staff or ring, into the hand of his creditor — was understood by medieval canonists as the secular echo of the covenants of Scripture. Pollock and Maitland trace the development of English contract through the action of debt, the action of covenant under seal, and the eventual emergence of assumpsit, and at every stage the moral and theological imagery hovers behind the procedural one. The seal on the deed was, for centuries, considered to import its own consideration: the gravity of having sworn before God was sufficient. The form was an imitation, however dim, of the form Abraham knew.

Augustine in the passage above is reading the Genesis covenant in light of its fulfillment in Christ. The land promised to Abraham, he says, is given — both literally to Israel and spiritually to the Church — and the God who promised has performed. The keeping is divine. Notice the move. Augustine is saying that the surety of every lesser promise — every covenant under heaven, including the ones in your filing cabinet — draws its dignity from the fact that the original covenant was kept. The reason it is meaningful to bind yourself by your word at all is that God bound himself first and was faithful to his binding. If the universe in which we make promises were a universe in which the first Promisor had defaulted, all lesser promises would lose their footing. We swear in a covenanted world. That is why our swearing has any weight.

Translate this into the room where your client is signing. The closing table is one of the moments in modern professional life where the covenant-form is, briefly, visible again. The parties gather. The instruments are arrayed. Each signer puts his name to a document that will commit him in ways he cannot fully foresee. The notary asks if he signs willingly. He says yes. The ink is dry. Something has been done that the law will enforce, that his name has been put behind, that the future will be measured against. This is, in miniature and in commercial register, what happened at Mamre. The signer may not know it; the lawyer may not pause to notice it; but the form is the form.

The covenant-form does several things contract, considered as a mere transactional mechanism, cannot do. It binds across time. It creates identity — a covenanted people, an incorporated entity, a married couple. It establishes a sign — a wedding ring, a corporate seal, a registered trademark, a deed recorded at the county. It contemplates faithlessness as a moral failure and not merely a default event. It carries with it, in its deep grammar, the possibility of being broken and the question of what is to be done when it is. The covenant-form, in short, knows it is dealing with persons, not only parties.

This week we will move from covenant to its more attenuated descendants — the bilateral commercial contract, the loan agreement, the settlement — and we will see how the moral substrate persists even as the form simplifies. But today, just sit with the original. The first lawyer's job was to remember a covenant. Moses with the tablets. Joshua at Shechem reading the law every seven years to the assembled people. Ezra at the rebuilt walls reading what had been forgotten. The line runs from there through the medieval scriveners to the closing room where, on Friday afternoon, you watch a founder sign a $20 million credit agreement and explain to him, patiently, what each covenant means.

You stand in a long line of people whose job was to be present when a covenant was made and to remember it afterward on behalf of those who might forget. The line begins with Abraham on his face. You are downstream of that scene. The dignity of your day comes from how far back the river runs.`,
