    meditation: `Why should the law care about promises? The question sounds almost adolescent — of course the law cares about promises; that is half of what the law is — but the question is in fact a deep one, and the answer the Western legal tradition gives to it is not obvious. Promises are made of breath. They have no inherent enforcement mechanism. If the promisor does not keep his word, no physical force compels him; the wronged party cannot reach into the promisor's mind and extract performance. Why, then, has nearly every developed legal order set up elaborate machinery — courts, judgments, sheriffs, levies, writs of execution — to compel the keeping of promises that, by their nature, depend on willing breath alone?

There are at least three answers, and a Christian lawyer ought to know each.

The first is Aristotelian and Thomistic. Promise-keeping is a virtue, and the law's purpose includes the cultivation of virtue. Aristotle, in the Nicomachean Ethics, argues that the polity exists not merely to keep the peace but to form citizens capable of the human goods, of which justice is the most political. Aquinas, building on him, treats law as ordained to virtue — pedagogical, not merely coercive. If promise-keeping is a virtue of justice, and if the law is among the instruments by which virtues are habituated, then the law's enforcement of contracts is part of the formation of a people who can keep their word. The enforced contract is the trellis on which the virtue of fidelity grows. Repeal contract law and you do not merely lose a useful commercial tool; you lose a school in which a generation learned that words have consequences and that a man's name on paper has weight.

The second answer is more pragmatic — the answer Blackstone gives in the passage above, in the gentle understated voice that English jurisprudence has used for these matters since Hooker. Once a contract is entered into, Blackstone says, it becomes a chose in action — a thing one does not yet have, but has the right to claim. The law's tenderness to that right is its raison d'etre in this domain. If a man's promise, freely given, could be lightly broken without redress, the entire economic and social order would shake. Trade would become impossible at any meaningful scale. Lending could not exist; you would never advance money you could not recover. Marriage would degenerate; the children of any household would live in perpetual contingency. Civic cooperation would corrode. The law honors promises because a society in which promises are not honored is not, in any thick sense, a society at all. It is a state of nature with paperwork.

Notice how Blackstone's language hovers near the Aquinas. The promise of a man, freely given, may not be lightly broken without redress. There is a moral cadence in the sentence — the word "lightly" doing serious work. Blackstone is not saying every promise must be enforced robotically. He is saying the law's posture toward promise is one of tenderness: protective of the right, suspicious of breach, prepared to grant remedies where the breach is unjustified. The common-law sensibility is essentially that of a careful gardener tending the institution by which strangers come to trust each other. The American casebooks descend from this sensibility, even where they have forgotten its theology.

The third answer is the theological one, which we touched yesterday and which we should not let slip behind the jurisprudential ones. The law honors promises because the universe was begun by a Promise — God's word that spoke being into nothing — and is sustained by a Promise — the new covenant in Christ's blood. The whole order of creation is a kept word. A society that does not honor promises is a society that has, in its small way, denied the structure of reality. Conversely, a society that honors them — clumsily, partially, sometimes wrongly — is still in some way aligned with the grain of the cosmos. Blackstone does not say this in the passage, because eighteenth-century English jurisprudence had largely stopped saying it aloud, but it is in the inheritance, and it is in the cathedral he wrote his Commentaries beside.

Now bring these three answers down to your Tuesday. You are drafting a non-compete for a sales executive. The reasons the law will enforce that non-compete are, layered: because keeping faith with your former employer is a virtue you should not be tempted out of for thirty pieces of silver; because if non-competes could not be enforced, businesses could not invest in training employees and the economy would suffer; and because the structure of reality is on the side of kept promises. The young lawyer who knows only the second reason will draft an instrument that is technically enforceable but morally thin. The lawyer who knows all three will draft something better — narrower in scope, more carefully tailored to the legitimate interest, less inclined to use the law as a club to extract performance the executive should not have to render. The depth of the rationale shapes the prudence of the drafting.

This matters because much of the modern law of contract has come unmoored from these older rationales and now floats on a thinner one — what scholars call the "efficient breach" theory. The theory, in its sharper forms, holds that the law's job in contract is merely to channel breach into damages so as to allow value-maximizing reallocations of resources. The promisor who can earn more by breaching than he loses by paying expectation damages should breach, and the law should not stand in his way. The theory is not stupid. It captures something the older theories sometimes missed, which is that promise-keeping cannot always be specifically enforced and that monetary compensation can in some cases do what the older equity could not. But the efficient-breach theory is dangerous when it becomes the only theory a lawyer has heard. It treats promise as priced behavior, treats breach as a routine business decision, and erodes — slowly — the cultural soil in which fidelity grows.

Henry Stimson, in his Popular Law-making, made the same point in plain American prose. The law of contract, he wrote, had developed because Anglo-American society had decided the keeping of one's word was a thing the community would back with force. Stimson insisted that the law was a teacher of fidelity, not merely an accountant of breach.

The point for the working lawyer is this. When you draft, you are participating in the law's pedagogical work. When you litigate a contract case, you are arguing not only for damages but for the cultural premise on which damages rest. None of this is small. The wages of treating it as small are the slow erosion of the very promise-keeping the law was built to honor.

The next time you find yourself drafting a contract — even a routine one — let three thoughts pass through you. The promise here is a virtue; my work is forming the person who makes it. The promise here is an institution; my work is tending the loom on which a society is woven. The promise here belongs, ultimately, to a covenanted world; my work is downstream of a God who keeps his word.

Then, holding all three, write the clause.`,
