    meditation: `The Latin maxim is one of the oldest in the law: pacta sunt servanda. Pacts are to be kept. The maxim is older than the Justinian Code, older than the canon law, older than Blackstone. It is the bone of contract jurisprudence in nearly every civilized order. And yet — and this is what an honest lawyer has to sit with — it has never been absolute, in any tradition that has thought carefully about it.

Cicero, in the passage above, is writing two thousand years ago, before Christ was born, and he is already qualifying. He affirms the maxim — the man who breaks a promise without reason is the very pattern of unfaithfulness. He commends fidelity. He treats lightness in promise-keeping as a moral disease. And then he names the exceptions. Promises to do wrong do not bind. Promises whose keeping would harm the promisee more than help him do not bind in the same way. Promises that, when made, contemplated circumstances which have catastrophically changed may have to be reconsidered. The Stoic tradition, with its dignity about kept words, is not naive. It understands that the world moves and that fidelity sometimes wears different clothes than first appeared.

The canon law took up Cicero's qualifications and theorized them. Aquinas, in the Summa, treats the binding force of promises and lists the conditions under which the obligation may yield: when the matter promised was inherently sinful, when circumstances change so radically that what is now being asked is essentially a different promise, when the promisee releases. The modern doctrines we know — frustration, impossibility, impracticability, force majeure — descend from this tradition. They are not modern inventions. They are the same intuitions, dressed in nineteenth- and twentieth-century clothes.

This is the territory the working lawyer most often enters in moments of real difficulty. The seven-year supply agreement signed when wheat was eight dollars a bushel; the war has come and wheat is forty; the buyer insists on the contract price; the seller will be ruined if he honors it. The lease signed in 2019 by a restaurant that has now lost half its dinner trade to delivery apps; the rent has not changed; the landlord points to the document; the tenant says the world that document assumed no longer exists. The settlement agreement that contemplated continuing performance by a party that has since died. The credit agreement that assumed an interest rate environment that has shifted by four hundred basis points. The marriage that became a divorce, where the prenuptial agreement now binds two people one of whom has changed in ways the eighteen-year-old version of herself could not have anticipated.

These are the cases where pacta sunt servanda meets reality. Most of the time, the maxim must prevail. The reason is the one we sat with yesterday: a society in which contracts can be undone whenever performance becomes inconvenient is a society in which no one will lend, no one will invest, no one will plan. The whole virtue of contract is precisely that it survives the change of mood, the change of weather, the change of incentive. If contracts only bound when convenient, they would not bind at all. The maxim is therefore the default, and the default is heavy. The presumption favors performance. The party seeking release bears a high burden.

But the burden is not infinite. The tradition has always recognized that there is a point at which the equities tip — where insistence on literal performance has become not faithfulness but cruelty, where what was promised has, in essence, become a different thing. The modern doctrine of frustration of purpose, the doctrine of impossibility, the doctrine of commercial impracticability, the equitable defense of unconscionability, the bankruptcy rejection of executory contracts — all are juridical mechanisms by which the law admits, with care, that the maxim has limits.

The lawyer's job in these moments is one of the hardest in practice. You are representing the founder who covenanted in 2021 to deliver a financial product that the regulatory landscape now forbids; he wants to be released; the counterparty wants the deal kept. You are representing the parent who promised in the custody decree to live within twenty miles of the other parent; the job that made that promise possible has disappeared; the new job is in another state; the agreement is silent. You are representing the long-time client who signed a personal guaranty in 2018 for a venture that has since failed; the bank is calling the guaranty; the man's house and his retirement are on the line; the loan documents are airtight and the bank's right is clear.

In each case, your work is twofold. First, to honor the maxim. The promise was made; the keeper of promises is your client; the default is performance. You sit with your client and you make sure he understands that. You do not dress up his self-interest in the language of moral excuse. You do not tell him the contract does not mean what it says when in fact it does. The first service the lawyer renders is to remind his client what he committed to. This is the unpopular half of the role, and it is essential.

Second, to identify the exceptions. Because Cicero is right, and Aquinas is right, and the common law is right: not every promise binds in every condition, and the legal tradition has built up — over two millennia — a careful catalog of when the bond yields. Frustration of purpose. Impracticability. Material adverse change. Equity. Bankruptcy. Statutory release. Each is narrow; each requires careful pleading; each requires the lawyer to do the work of distinguishing self-serving regret from genuine equitable claim. But the doctrines exist, and they exist because the tradition has known, since before Christ, that fidelity has a shape and is not mere literalism.

The temptation in modern practice is to collapse the structure in one of two ways. The first is rigorism — treat every contract as absolutely binding, refuse to entertain release, watch your client be ground to dust by a deal that no one foresaw. The second is laxity — treat every contract as renegotiable when the wind changes, advise your client to walk whenever the math favors it. Both miss what the tradition has been trying to hold.

The tradition has been trying to hold this: a promise binds. It binds because of who the promisor is and because of the kind of universe we are in. It binds even when keeping it is hard. And — here is the part the rigorist forgets — it does not always bind. There is a category of change so radical, of equity so disturbed, that the promise has, in essence, become something the original parties did not agree to. In that narrow category, the lawyer's office is to help the court see what fidelity now requires.

It is the most Solomon-like part of contract practice. You are sorting which side of the line your case falls on. You are doing it not with a formula but with judgment, which is what Aristotle said practical wisdom required and what Aquinas said prudence supplied. You are, in the deepest sense, exercising equity inside the law of contract.

Pray, before you advise your next client on a release-from-contract question, that you neither flatter his desire to walk nor flatter the counterparty's desire to grind. The maxim is heavy; the exceptions are real; the line between is your work.`,
    prayer:
      "Lord of the kept word, you bind us by your promises and you also know when our promises must yield. Make me a lawyer who honors the maxim and recognizes the exception. Keep me from flattering my client out of his obligations and from flattering my opponent into rigorism. Give me the prudence to know which case is which.",
    prompt:
      "Which open matter on your desk involves a promise whose binding force is genuinely contested? Have you done the work of distinguishing your client's self-interest from his legitimate equitable claim?",
    situationalTags: ["settlement-negotiation"],
